For example, a user might request that a 2 MB PDF be reduced to approximately 250 KB.

Implement a sensible iterative compression strategy where technically possible.

Requirements:

* Determine whether the PDF contains primarily images, vector content, or text.
* Preserve searchable text and vector content whenever possible.
* Prefer lossless or minimally destructive optimizations when suitable.
* Use image downsampling and quality adjustments only when appropriate.
* Allow users to choose a quality-versus-size trade-off.
* Avoid rasterizing every page by default.
* Do not claim to achieve an impossible target.
* Stop retrying when further compression is unlikely to help.
* Display the actual original and output sizes.
* Display the real percentage reduction.
* If the target is not achievable, clearly explain this and provide the best valid result when appropriate.
* Never claim that a target size was achieved when it was not.
* Ensure the generated file remains a valid PDF.

If a browser-only approach cannot reliably perform a particular compression operation, identify the limitation instead of pretending the operation is equivalent to professional native compression tools.

Use native tools only if the deployment architecture can support them reliably.

